{"parentUuid":"2018c1af-f54b-4c71-84ff-30bc97fe5e46","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b8557a2-9430-4f17-946a-1ea07dab5643","agentId":"a5206b5b2ad4bea59","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Si3iXJT87t4FzxDoTjy9FV","type":"tool_result","content":"commit f860ff366b50328d62a3ff06a1cd76d39e84245f\nAuthor: Mykyta Svyrydenko <<redacted-email>>\nDate:   Sat Oct 3 16:44:15 2026 +0200\n\n    codex: bash-tool-result cell supported, recorded in runs/shell-exit-status (#91)\n    \n    Codex tells the agent a failed command's output like a success's; the failure is only in the event stream's command_execution item (status failed, exit_code). Recorded with the real codex; the cell lists that as a deviation, and the mock does the same.\n    \n    Co-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\ndiff --git a/codex-mock/snapshots/runs/shell-exit-status/run.yaml b/codex-mock/snapshots/runs/shell-exit-status/run.yaml\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..6c879f7\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/codex-mock/snapshots/runs/shell-exit-status/run.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,2 @@\n+version: 0.159.3\n+command: codex exec --json --skip-git-repo-check --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox --dangerously-bypass-hook-trust -m gpt-5.6-luna\ndiff --git a/spec/capabilities/bash-tool-result.yaml b/spec/capabilities/bash-tool-result.yaml\nindex 0093ac4..c94ef36 100644\n--- a/spec/capabilities/bash-tool-result.yaml\n+++ b/spec/capabilities/bash-tool-result.yaml\n@@ -13,7 +13,21 @@ providers:\n     runs:\n       - claude-mock/snapshots/runs/bashfail\n       - claude-mock/snapshots/runs/stops\n-  codex: pending\n+  codex:\n+    docs:\n+      - https://developers.openai.com/codex/hooks#posttooluse\n+    runs:\n+      - codex-mock/snapshots/runs/shell-exit-status\n+    deviations:\n+      - adr: capability-grounding\n+        statement: >-\n+          Codex does not make a failed command an error result for the agent:\n+          what it is told is the command's output alone, the same text a\n+          success would have, with no exit code stated and none marked as an\n+          error (recorded: runs/shell-exit-status). The failure is shown only\n+          in the event stream's command_execution item, whose status is\n+          \"failed\" and whose exit_code and aggregated_output carry the exit\n+          status and what the command printed, and the mock does the same.\n   cursor:\n     docs:\n       - https://cursor.com/docs/hooks#aftershellexecution\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# The ONLY writer of codex-mock/snapshots/ (the snapshots-read-only gate\n# refuses every other write). Everything it writes is sealed; snapshots-current\n# refuses a snapshot whose seal does not match, so a hand edit is caught at the\n# commit even if it slipped past the gate.\n#\n#   capture.sh run <name>    run the scenario in runs/<name>/setup/ against the\n#                            real `codex exec`, and add a sample\n#   capture.sh doc <url>     freeze a doc page (e.g. https://developers.openai.com/codex/hooks):\n#                            its sha256 goes in the MANIFEST; its text only into a cache\n#                            under the git dir, never into the repo\n#   capture.sh drop <run> <ts>  remove one sample (a bad or non-hermetic capture)\n#   capture.sh all           re-capture every run and doc at the installed\n#                            codex's version, and set MANIFEST.version to it\n#\n# A scenario is hand-authored, and only its setup/ is:\n#   runs/<name>/setup/prompt.txt       the prompt\n#   runs/<name>/setup/hooks.json       the user layer's $CODEX_HOME/hooks.json\n#   runs/<name>/setup/hook.sh          optional: the hook every event runs;\n#                                      it appends its stdin to $HOOK_LOG\n#   runs/<name>/setup/args             optional: extra codex exec flags, one per line\n#   runs/<name>/setup/env              optional: KEY=VALUE lines codex inherits on\n#                                      top of the hermetic env (e.g. an outer session's)\n#\n# Authentication: codex has no headless login but the user's. The run's fake\n# CODEX_HOME holds ONLY a symlink to the logged-in auth.json (the same trick as\n# claude-mock's Keychains link): nothing is copied, and a token codex refreshes\n# during the run is moved back over the original, so the login stays valid.\n# The link is gone with the fake home, and a capture is refused if any token\n# value reached the sample.\nset -euo pipefail\nhere=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\" && pwd)\"\nmanifest=\"$here/MANIFEST.yaml\"\ndie() { echo \"capture.sh: $*\" >&2; exit 1; }\nversion() { yq -r '.version' \"$manifest\"; }\ninstalled() { codex --version | awk '{print $2}'; }\nauth_src=\"${CODEX_AUTH_JSON:-$HOME/.codex/auth.json}\"\n# The first capture creates the MANIFEST, frozen at the installed version: it is\n# a snapshot file too, so nothing else may write it.\n[ -f \"$manifest\" ] || printf 'version: \"%s\"\\n' \"$(installed)\" >\"$manifest\"\n\n# One capture at a time: they are real model calls on one account.\nlock=/tmp/capture-codex.lock.d\nacquire() { local i=0; until mkdir \"$lock\" 2>/dev/null; do i=$((i + 1)); [ \"$i\" -le 600 ] || die \"another capture holds $lock\"; sleep 1; done; }\n\n# seal DIR — SEAL lists the sha256 of every other file under DIR.\n# Written to SEAL.tmp and renamed only on success, so a SEAL always means a\n# complete sample (the cleanup trap relies on it).\nseal() { (cd \"$1\" && find . -type f ! -name 'SEAL*' | LC_ALL=C sort | xargs shasum -a 256 >SEAL.tmp && mv SEAL.tmp SEAL); }\n\n# normalize — hook payloads and event-stream frames into events.jsonl: what a\n# scenario did, without what differs between two captures of the same\n# behaviour (ids, paths, timings, the model's own wording).\n# The run's own session (thread) id is kept as <SESSION_ID> wherever it appears\n# (a payload's session_id, a child's env, a command's output): that they match\n# is recorded, the value itself is not.\nnormalize() {\n  local cap=\"$1\" sid\n  sid=\"$(jq -r 'select(.session_id) | .session_id' \"$cap/payloads.jsonl\" 2>/dev/null | head -n1 || true)\"\n  jq -c --arg sid \"${sid:-<none>}\" 'walk(if type == \"object\" then del(.transcript_path, .cwd, .turn_id, .tool_use_id, .uuid,\ncodex-mock/snapshots/runs:\nhook-exit-codes\nhook-exit-json\nhook-matchers\nhook-timeout\nhook-unstartable\nnested-session-env\nposttool-block\npretool-decisions\nprompt-blocked\nsession-end\nshell-exit-status\nstops\nsubprocess-session-env\n\ncursor-mock/snapshots/runs:\nadditional-context\nfile-tools\nhook-exit-codes\nhook-fail-closed\nhook-json-nonzero\nhook-matchers\nhook-timeout\nhooks-together\nnested-session-env\npretool-refusal\nsession-start-block\nshell-exit-status\nsubprocess-session-env\nsymlinked-cwd\ntool-failure\nhook.sh\nprompt.txt\nsettings.json\nUse the Agent tool with subagent_type general-purpose, description 'sleeper', run_in_background true, and prompt: 'Run the Bash command: sleep 5; echo SUBDONE . Then reply with the single word SUBREPLY.' After launching it, immediately end your turn by replying only LAUNCHED. Do not wait.statement: >-\n  A foreground shell command's result carries its output and a structured form\n  of it; a command that exits non-zero is an error result carrying what the\n  command printed and reporting its exit code (a harness may state the exit\n  code only when the command printed nothing). (Where a harness knows that exit status 1 only reports\n  no match or a difference (a search, a comparison), output limits, timeouts,\n  what a command leaves behind for the next one, and commands run in the\n  background are not part of this.)\nproviders:\n  claude:\n    docs:\n      - https://code.claude.com/docs/en/tools-reference#bash-tool-behavior\n    runs:\n      - claude-mock/snapshots/runs/bashfail\n      - claude-mock/snapshots/runs/stops\n  codex:\n    docs:\n      - https://developers.openai.com/codex/hooks#posttooluse\n    runs:\n      - codex-mock/snapshots/runs/shell-exit-status\n    deviations:\n      - adr: capability-grounding\n        statement: >-\n          Codex does not make a failed command an error result for the agent:\n          what it is told is the command's output alone, the same text a\n          success would have, with no exit code stated and none marked as an\n          error (recorded: runs/shell-exit-status). The failure is shown only\n          in the event stream's command_execution item, whose status is\n          \"failed\" and whose exit_code and aggregated_output carry the exit\n          status and what the command printed, and the mock does the same.\n  cursor:\n    docs:\n      - https://cursor.com/docs/hooks#aftershellexecution\n      - https://cursor.com/docs/hooks#posttoolusefailure\n    runs:\n      - cursor-mock/snapshots/runs/shell-exit-status\n      - cursor-mock/snapshots/runs/tool-failure","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d0c2b02c-8527-48ba-acae-2a926c7a37b8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:46:28.657Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2018c1af-f54b-4c71-84ff-30bc97fe5e46","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/nsviridenko/ws/sloprail/harness-mocks/.claude/worktrees/agent-a5206b5b2ad4bea59","sessionId":"f103aecd-8ff6-40a5-ad21-f5bd02338666","version":"2.1.286","gitBranch":"claude/capabilities-matrix-impl-2a0cfd"}
{"parentUuid":"e8a73900-b8d9-4d5a-9ff0-b9947678145e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b8557a2-9430-4f17-946a-1ea07dab5643","agentId":"a5206b5b2ad4bea59","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PqZWnqpwkiBgeBvLeY54qc","type":"tool_result","content":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# The ONLY writer of cursor-mock/snapshots/ (the snapshots-read-only gate\n# refuses every other write). Everything it writes is sealed; snapshots-current\n# refuses a snapshot whose seal does not match, so a hand edit is caught at the\n# commit even if it slipped past the gate.\n#\n#   capture.sh run <name>    run the scenario in runs/<name>/setup/ against the\n#                            real `cursor-agent`, and add a sample\n#   capture.sh doc <url>     freeze a doc page (e.g. https://cursor.com/docs/hooks):\n#                            its sha256 goes in the MANIFEST; its text only into a cache\n#                            under the git dir, never into the repo\n#   capture.sh drop <run> <ts>  remove one sample (a bad or non-hermetic capture)\n#   capture.sh all           re-capture every run and doc at the installed\n#                            cursor-agent's version, and set MANIFEST.version to it\n#\n# The version is cursor-agent's release date (`2026.09.28`): `--version` also\n# prints a build hash after it (`2026.09.28-64d2043`), which the schema's\n# version pattern does not take, so the hash is dropped.\n#\n# Login: cursor-agent has no environment-only login in this setup, so the fake\n# HOME links to the user's Keychains directory and nothing else (the same as\n# claude-mock's capture). No credential is copied into the fake HOME; the\n# email the login reports is nulled out of every payload before anything is\n# sealed. A CURSOR_API_KEY is never read or passed.\n#\n# A scenario is hand-authored, and only its setup/ is:\n#   runs/<name>/setup/prompt.txt       the prompt\n#   runs/<name>/setup/hooks.json       the project's .cursor/hooks.json\n#   runs/<name>/setup/*.sh             optional: the hook scripts, installed in .cursor/hooks/;\n#                                      hook.sh appends to $HOOK_LOG\n#   runs/<name>/setup/args             optional: extra cursor-agent flags, one per line\n#   runs/<name>/setup/env              optional: KEY=VALUE lines cursor-agent inherits on\n#                                      top of the hermetic env\nset -euo pipefail\nhere=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\" && pwd)\"\nmanifest=\"$here/MANIFEST.yaml\"\ndie() { echo \"capture.sh: $*\" >&2; exit 1; }\nversion() { yq -r '.version' \"$manifest\"; }\ninstalled() { cursor-agent --version | awk '{print $1}' | sed 's/-.*$//'; }\n# The first capture creates the MANIFEST, frozen at the installed version: it is\n# a snapshot file too, so nothing else may write it.\n[ -f \"$manifest\" ] || printf 'version: \"%s\"\\n' \"$(installed)\" >\"$manifest\"\n\n# seal DIR — SEAL lists the sha256 of every other file under DIR.\n# Written to SEAL.tmp and renamed only on success, so a SEAL always means a\n# complete sample (the cleanup trap relies on it).\nseal() { (cd \"$1\" && find . -type f ! -name 'SEAL*' | LC_ALL=C sort | xargs shasum -a 256 >SEAL.tmp && mv SEAL.tmp SEAL); }\n\n# normalize — hook payloads and stream frames into events.jsonl: what a\n# scenario did, without what differs between two captures of the same\ncat: adr/capability-grounding: Is a directory\ncodex-cli 0.159.3\nCodex CLI\n\nIf no subcommand is specified, options will be forwarded to the interactive CLI.\n\nUsage: codex [OPTIONS] [PROMPT]\n       codex [OPTIONS] <COMMAND> [ARGS]\n\nCommands:\n  agents            Browse all agent sessions on the shared local app-server daemon\n  exec              Run Codex non-interactively [aliases: e]\n  review            Run a code review non-interactively\n  login             Manage login\n  logout            Remove stored authentication credentials\n  mcp               Manage external MCP servers for Codex\n  plugin            Manage Codex plugins\n  app-server        [experimental] Run the app server or related tooling\n  remote-control    [experimental] Manage the app-server daemon with remote control enabled\n  app               Launch the Desktop app (opens the app installer if missing)\n  completion        Generate shell completion scripts\n  update            Update Codex to the latest version\n  doctor            Diagnose local Codex installation, config, auth, and runtime health\n  sandbox           Run commands within a Codex-provided sandbox\n  debug             Debugging tools\n  apply             Apply the latest diff produced by Codex agent as a `git apply` to your local\n                    working tree [aliases: a]\n  resume            Resume a previous interactive session (picker by default; use --last to continue\n                    the most recent)\n  queue             Queue a message for an existing session\n  archive           Archive a saved session by id or session name\n  delete            Permanently delete a saved session by id or session name\n  migrate-rollouts  Inspect or migrate legacy local sessions to paginated thread history\n  unarchive         Unarchive a saved session by id or session name\n  fork              Fork a previous interactive session (picker by default; use --last to fork the\n                    most recent)\n  cloud             [EXPERIMENTAL] Browse tasks from Codex Cloud and apply changes locally\n  exec-server       [EXPERIMENTAL] Run the standalone exec-server service\n  features          Inspect feature flags\n  help              Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)\n\nArguments:\nagent_message_board                      under development  false\nanalytics_plan_history                   experimental       false\napi_key_model_discovery                  under development  false\napply_patch_freeform                     removed            false\napply_patch_preserve_line_endings        under development  false\napply_patch_streaming_events             under development  false\napps                                     stable             true\napps_mcp_path_override                   removed            false\nartifact                                 under development  false\nauth_elicitation                         stable             true\nbackground_paginated_rollout_migration   under development  false\nbedrock_setup_wizard                     under development  false\nbrowser_use                              stable             true\nbrowser_use_external                     stable             true\nbrowser_use_full_cdp_access              stable             true\nchronicle                                under development  false\ncode_mode                                under development  false\ncode_mode_buffered_exec                  removed            false\ncode_mode_host                           stable             true\ncode_mode_interrupt                      under development  false\ncode_mode_only                           under development  false\ncode_mode_prewarm                        under development  false\ncodex_apps_mcp_2026_07_28                under development  false\ncodex_git_commit                         removed            false\ncollaboration_modes                      removed            true\ncompaction_image_budget                  stable             true\ncomputer_use                             stable             true\nconcurrent_reasoning_summaries           under development  false\ncontent_item_kinds                       stable             true\ncontext_management                       under development  false\ncurrent_time_reminder                    under development  false\ncwd_relative_turn_diffs                  under development  false\ndaemon_auto_start                        stable             true\ndefault_mode_request_user_input          under development  false\ndefer_mailbox_preemption                 under development  false\ndeferred_executor                        under development  false\ndeferred_tool_world_state                under development  false\nelevated_windows_sandbox                 removed            false\nenable_fanout                            removed            false\nenable_mcp_apps                          under development  false","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"2c43d7b1-c8a9-4a12-bf5e-537c27a32712","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:46:46.358Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e8a73900-b8d9-4d5a-9ff0-b9947678145e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/nsviridenko/ws/sloprail/harness-mocks/.claude/worktrees/agent-a5206b5b2ad4bea59","sessionId":"f103aecd-8ff6-40a5-ad21-f5bd02338666","version":"2.1.286","gitBranch":"claude/capabilities-matrix-impl-2a0cfd"}
